public with sharing class ContactDomainService {

    public static Map<String, List<Contact>> groupByEmailDomain(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        Map<String, List<Contact>> result = new Map<String, List<Contact>>();

        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Email, AccountId
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
              AND Email != null
              AND Email != ''
        ];

        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            String email = c.Email;
            Integer atIndex = email.indexOf('@');
            if (atIndex == -1 || atIndex == email.length() - 1) {
                continue;
            }

            String domain = email.substring(atIndex + 1).toLowerCase();
            if (domain.isEmpty()) {
                continue;
            }

            List<Contact> bucket = result.get(domain);
            if (bucket == null) {
                bucket = new List<Contact>();
                result.put(domain, bucket);
            }
            bucket.add(c);
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

**Key design decisions:**

| Concern | How it's handled |
|---|---|
| **Sharing** | `with sharing` on the class guarantees every SOQL enforces the running user's sharing rules regardless of the caller's sharing mode. |
| **Null / empty input** | Early return of an empty map before any DML or SOQL. |
| **Contacts with no email** | Filtered out in the SOQL predicate (`Email != null AND Email != ''`), so they never enter the heap. |
| **Malformed email (no `@`)** | Guarded in Apex: `indexOf('@') == -1` or `@` is the last character → skipped. |
| **Case-normalisation** | `toLowerCase()` on the extracted domain ensures `Jane@Example.COM` maps to `example.com`. |
| **Field minimisation** | Only `Id`, `Email`, `AccountId` are selected, reducing heap usage and avoiding unnecessary field-level security checks. |
| **Bulk-safe** | The method accepts a `Set<Id>` and issues a single SOQL; no per-record queries. |

