```apex
public class AccountOnboardingService {

    public static List<Account> createAccountsWithContacts(Map<String, List<String>> contactLastNamesByAccountName) {
        if (contactLastNamesByAccountName == null || contactLastNamesByAccountName.isEmpty()) {
            return new List<Account>();
        }

        fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork uow = new fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork();

        // Phase 1 – queue and insert Accounts so their Ids are populated
        List<Account> accounts = new List<Account>();
        for (String accountName : contactLastNamesByAccountName.keySet()) {
            accounts.add(new Account(Name = accountName));
        }
        uow.insertObject(accounts);
        uow.commitWork();

        // Phase 2 – queue and insert Contacts now that Account Ids are known
        List<Contact> contacts = new List<Contact>();
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            List<String> lastNames = contactLastNamesByAccountName.get(acc.Name);
            if (lastNames != null) {
                for (String lastName : lastNames) {
                    contacts.add(new Contact(LastName = lastName, AccountId = acc.Id));
                }
            }
        }
        if (!contacts.isEmpty()) {
            uow.insertObject(contacts);
            uow.commitWork();
        }

        return accounts;
    }
}
```

**Why two `commitWork()` calls?**  
A `Contact.AccountId` must reference an already-existing Account record at DML time. Because `fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork` batches inserts per SObject type in a single DML statement (it does not resolve cross-object Id dependencies automatically), the Accounts must be committed first so their Ids are populated on the SObject instances. The Contacts are then queued with the now-known `AccountId` values and committed in a second bulk DML. Both phases stay within the same `fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork` instance, no raw DML is written, and each DML statement handles all records of its type in one call—keeping the method bulk-safe for hundreds of contacts.

